<article class="env-lemma" id="0DX9">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0DX9">Lemma <span data-tag="0DX9">51.4.3</span>.</a> Let $I \subset A$ be a finitely generated ideal of a ring $A$. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$\text{cd}(A, I)$ is at most equal to the number of generators of $I$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\text{cd}(A, I) \leq r$ if there exist $f_1, \ldots , f_ r \in A$ such that $V(f_1, \ldots , f_ r) = V(I)$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\text{cd}(A, I) \leq c$ if $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) \setminus V(I)$ can be covered by $c$ affine opens. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The explicit description for $R\Gamma _ Y(-)$ given in Dualizing Complexes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0A6R" data-tag="0A6R">47.9.1</a> shows that (1) is true. We can deduce (2) from (1) using the fact that $R\Gamma _ Z$ depends only on the closed subset $Z$ and not on the choice of the finitely generated ideal $I \subset A$ with $V(I) = Z$. This follows either from the construction of local cohomology in Dualizing Complexes, Section <a href="/tag/0952" data-tag="0952">47.9</a> combined with More on Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0953" data-tag="0953">15.90.6</a> or it follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0A6T" data-tag="0A6T">51.2.1</a>. To see (3) we use Lemma <a href="/tag/0DX7" data-tag="0DX7">51.4.1</a> and the vanishing result of Cohomology of Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01XI" data-tag="01XI">30.4.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>